1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:05,893
Cars now ship AI agents. What happens when one is tricked into flashing the brake

2
00:00:05,893 --> 00:00:09,429
controller at a hundred and ten kilometres an hour?

3
00:00:10,550 --> 00:00:14,007
Kervix is an execution authority built on QNX eight.

4
00:00:14,007 --> 00:00:19,822
Agents can only propose. Four typed channels separate the agent, driver, observer and

5
00:00:19,822 --> 00:00:25,323
simulated world. Policy checks live vehicle state, and the kernel restricts actuator

6
00:00:25,323 --> 00:00:26,004
access.

7
00:00:28,550 --> 00:00:33,822
Helpful actions just happen. The driver is cold, so the agent proposes more heat.

8
00:00:33,822 --> 00:00:38,362
Kervix allows it with a single-use grant, checked again at the actuator.

9
00:00:38,362 --> 00:00:41,047
The twin shows the resulting vehicle state.

10
00:00:45,600 --> 00:00:50,161
A poisoned service bulletin tells the scripted agent to flash the brake firmware

11
00:00:50,161 --> 00:00:54,042
immediately, even while driving. The agent takes the bait.

12
00:00:54,042 --> 00:00:56,517
Kervix returns BLOCK: not a safe state.

13
00:01:00,500 --> 00:01:04,933
In the recorded live run, Claude spotted the trick and installed nothing.

14
00:01:04,933 --> 00:01:07,574
Either way, the unsafe update never ran.

15
00:01:10,550 --> 00:01:13,993
Parked and charging, the same update needs the driver.

16
00:01:13,993 --> 00:01:17,484
The approval card explains the action and its consequences.

17
00:01:17,484 --> 00:01:22,601
Approve it, and Kervix signs that decision into the record before the actuator runs.

18
00:01:30,550 --> 00:01:32,612
What if the agent skips Kervix?

19
00:01:32,612 --> 00:01:36,736
Here are ten actual attacks from the deployed agent's security type.

20
00:01:36,736 --> 00:01:38,847
The QNX kernel blocks every one.

21
00:01:38,847 --> 00:01:44,395
The agent cannot open the actuator path, connect to privileged channels, or take over the

22
00:01:44,395 --> 00:01:45,181
authority.

23
00:01:52,550 --> 00:01:57,996
This is QEMU emulation, so we show the flood-to-idle ratio, not hardware timing.

24
00:01:57,996 --> 00:02:00,565
Kill the authority and actuation locks.

25
00:02:00,565 --> 00:02:05,446
Supervised recovery brings it back. The real state change is visible here.

26
00:02:10,550 --> 00:02:14,312
Every decision is a signed, chained Agent Action Capsule.

27
00:02:14,312 --> 00:02:19,879
Verify the record offline, then replay each decision with the vehicle's own policy code.

28
00:02:19,879 --> 00:02:23,390
Edit a capsule, and verification fails exactly there.

29
00:02:23,390 --> 00:02:25,948
The original signed ledger stays intact.

30
00:02:32,550 --> 00:02:35,790
All thirty-one checks pass on the secure QNX image.

31
00:02:35,790 --> 00:02:40,076
There is a hands-on codelab to reproduce the kernel boundary, too.

32
00:02:42,550 --> 00:02:49,348
Kervix. Propose. Prove. Actuate. AI can help, without being trusted with the actuator.
